<TopicPage slug="subjects/occupational-therapy/foundational/occupation-the-core-construct">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Occupational Therapy and it needs zero prior knowledge — no
    anatomy, no psychology, no clinical vocabulary: every term is defined as it appears, and
    the whole construct is built from everyday activities you already do, like making a cup
    of tea.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what occupational therapists mean by "occupation" — self-care, productivity and leisure — and why it is not a job title;</li>
    <li>say why occupation is simultaneously the means, the end and the measure of the profession;</li>
    <li>distinguish the field's two dominant models, MOHO and CMOP-E, and state the question each answers;</li>
    <li>dissect an everyday activity into its demand structure: objects, sequence and timing, required body functions, and social, cultural and safety demands;</li>
    <li>use grading and adapting as two deliberate moves — and explain what occupational deprivation reveals about why occupation matters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The word "occupation" misleads every newcomer. In occupational therapy it does not mean a
    job — it means <strong>everything people do to occupy themselves: the self-care they must
    do, the productivity they choose, the leisure they love</strong>. Dressing, cooking,
    parenting, commuting, gaming, praying, working. The founding insight of the profession is
    that these ordinary activities are not just goals of health care but active ingredients in
    it: doing shapes health, identity and capability. Where medicine repairs the mechanism, OT
    restores the person's ability to do what matters to them — and when the mechanism cannot be
    fully repaired, it redesigns the task, the tool or the environment instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    A comparison makes the stance concrete. Two people recover from the same stroke with the same
    weak arm. The first is a retired accountant whose passion is baking bread; the second
    couldn't care less about the kitchen but lives for Sunday chess at the club. Exercise the
    identical arm identically in both cases and you get identical impairments — and two very
    different lives. Occupational therapy will still train grip, reach and endurance, but it will
    train them <em>through</em> kneading dough for one client and turning chess-clock buttons and
    carrying the board bag for the other, and it will ask what each person must be able to
    <em>do</em> to be themselves again. That is the difference between exercising a body part and
    rehabilitating a person: the occupation — not the muscle — is the organising unit. Occupation
    is the core construct because it is simultaneously the <em>means</em> (the therapeutic medium),
    the <em>end</em> (the goal the client names) and the <em>measure</em> (the outcome actually
    counted: can this person do what they need and love to do?).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="activity analysis: one cup of tea, dissected into its demand structure" widget="equationStep" config={teaCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the walk-through treats "make a cup of tea" as a chain of
    four demands, not one vague task. The objects set the physical challenge (a 2 kg full kettle
    versus a 300 ml one); the sequence shows where a stroke survivor's neglect or a memory
    impairment breaks the chain; the body functions are the lines a therapist can grade up or
    down; and the social and safety layer is why occupation is never just biomechanics. Change
    any one demand and you have adapted — or graded — the occupation.
  </p>

  <h2>What occupation is</h2>
  <p>
    The profession sorts occupations into families that structure both assessment and goals.
    <strong>Activities of daily living</strong> (ADLs) are the self-care basics: bathing, dressing,
    feeding, toileting, functional mobility. <strong>Instrumental ADLs</strong> are the complex
    domestic and community tasks independent living requires: cooking, cleaning, managing
    finances, using transport, taking medications correctly, shopping, using the phone.
    Beyond these sit <strong>work and education</strong>, <strong>play and leisure</strong>,
    <strong>social participation</strong>, and <strong>rest and sleep</strong> — the full span of
    a life, not merely the hospital ward. Two properties make an activity an <em>occupation</em>
    rather than a mere task: it is <em>personally meaningful</em>, and it occupies a place in a
    person's roles and routines. "Making breakfast" is also "being a mother before the school
    run"; the meaning is part of the activity's structure, which is why the same action can be
    effortless for one person and crushing for another.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each occupation by its family" widget="sort-buckets" config={otFamilySort} />
  <p>
    The history explains the conviction. The profession was founded in 1917 at Clifton Springs,
    New York, by a group including a psychiatrist, an architect and social workers, on the
    observation that filled time and purposeful activity measurably helped institutionalised
    patients — "occupation" as moral treatment, long before randomised trials. Adolf Meyer's 1922
    manifesto framed mental health as the balance of work, rest and play; Mary Reilly's 1961
    Eleanor Clarke Slagle Lecture bet that occupational therapy could become "one of the great
    ideas of twentieth-century medicine"; and in 1989 Elizabeth Yerxa and colleagues founded
    <strong>occupational science</strong>, the basic research discipline studying what humans do,
    why, and what it does to them — giving the applied profession the science an applied field
    needs. The timeline below traces the century.
  </p>
  <p>
    The construct has a dark mirror that proves its power: <strong>occupational deprivation</strong>.
    Refugees who cannot work in their host country, prisoners in unstructured confinement, elders
    moved into institutions where no one cooks or gardens — across these settings the loss of
    meaningful occupation reliably produces declines in mood, identity, skill and health,
    independent of physical condition. Occupations, in other words, behave like a need, not a
    luxury. That observation scales into <strong>occupational justice</strong> — the argument that
    opportunities to participate in meaningful occupation are a matter of fairness — and it is
    why the profession's remit stretches from an ICU bedside to housing policy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag between the assumption and the dark mirror" widget="compare" config={otDepriveCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that occupational therapy is "getting patients to do hobbies", or that it is the same as physiotherapy with different equipment. The distinction is the unit of analysis: physiotherapy's primary construct is movement and physical function; OT's is <em>engagement in occupation</em>. A physiotherapist strengthens shoulder abductors; an occupational therapist asks which occupation the client needs that shoulder for — feeding a cat, painting, signing documents — then analyses, grades and adapts <em>that occupation</em>, using exercise as one tool among many, never as the goal itself.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> occupation spans ADLs, instrumental ADLs, work, leisure, social
    participation and rest; an activity is an occupation when it is personally meaningful and
    holds a place in roles and routines; and deprivation of it measurably harms mood, identity,
    skill and health. Next, the field's two shared lenses for thinking it through.
  </p>

  <h2>Models of practice: MOHO and CMOP-E</h2>
  <p>
    Models are the field's shared thinking tools — lenses that tell a therapist what to observe
    and what to change. Two dominate the entry-level curriculum. The <strong>Model of Human
    Occupation</strong> (MOHO; Kielhofner and Burke, 1980, refined across four editions) explains
    doing as the output of three interacting subsystems embedded in an environment. <em>Volition</em>
    is the motivation to act: personal causation (what I believe I can do), values (what matters)
    and interests (what draws me). <em>Habituation</em> is the pattern of action: habits and
    internalised roles (worker, parent, student) that make behaviour semi-automatic.
    <em>Performance capacity</em> is what body and mind can actually deliver, moment to moment.
    MOHO's clinical power is diagnostic speech: it lets a therapist distinguish the patient who
    <em>cannot</em> dress (performance capacity), the one who <em>will not</em> (volition —
    perhaps dressing has become humiliation) and the one whose morning simply has no structure
    since retirement (habituation) — three different problems with three different therapies.
    Its assessments (the OCAIRS interview, the OPHI-II narrative) and its interventions
    (remotivation of choice, rebuilding habits and roles) all follow those subsystems.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>Canadian Model of Occupational Performance and Engagement (CMOP-E)</strong>
    (CAOT, 1997; extended 2007) is a picture of a relationship: a person — cognitive, affective
    and physical domains around a core of spirituality — at the centre of a triangle of
    <em>occupation</em> (self-care, productivity, leisure), nested within
    <strong>environments</strong>: cultural, institutional, physical and social. The transverse
    strip where person, occupation and world intersect is occupational performance and
    engagement. Its signature contribution is the <em>person–environment–occupation fit</em>
    idea: dysfunction is not a property of the person alone but of the mismatch — and the
    mismatch can be relieved from any corner. A delivery driver with lower-back pain can be
    strengthened (person), re-taught lifting (occupation), or re-equipped with a trolley and a
    redesigned route (environment) — three legitimate therapies for one problem, chosen by fit,
    evidence and the client's own priorities (the Canadian Occupational Performance Measure
    exists precisely to elicit those priorities and score change on them).
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the CMOP-E note" widget="spot-error" config={otCmopSpot} />
  <p>
    Used together, the models divide the labour. MOHO answers "why does this person not do?"
    (motivation, habits, capacity); CMOP-E answers "where is the mismatch?" (person, task or
    environment). Both push the therapist away from the impairment obsession that medical
    settings breed, toward the outcome the client actually experiences: doing. The WHO's ICF
    (2001) — classifying health as functioning and participation in context — gave the wider
    health system a vocabulary for exactly this shift, which is why occupation-centred practice
    reads as contemporary rather than eccentric.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag between the two models' questions" widget="compare" config={otModelsCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> MOHO answers "why does this person not do?" through volition,
    habituation and performance capacity; CMOP-E answers "where is the mismatch?" among person,
    occupation and environment — and the mismatch can be relieved from any corner. The timeline
    below shows where both models came from.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a century of occupation-centred thinking — scrub from the 1917 founding to CMOP-E" widget="timeline" config={otTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the century moves from conviction (the 1917 founding) to
    philosophy (Meyer, 1922) to wager (Reilly, 1961) to theory (MOHO, 1980) to science
    (occupational science, 1989) to model (CMOP, 1997; CMOP-E, 2007) — with the WHO's ICF in
    2001 giving the wider health system a vocabulary occupation-centred practice already spoke.
    Scrub the years and watch one construct hold steady while its instruments multiply.
  </p>

  <h2>Activity analysis: the profession's signature skill</h2>
  <p>
    The tea walk-through above is the skill in miniature: take any occupation apart into
    <strong>objects and their properties</strong> (weights, sizes, lids, temperatures),
    <strong>sequence and timing</strong> (which step, which order, where the waiting is),
    <strong>required body functions</strong> (grip, reach, standing tolerance, vision, memory,
    temperature sense) and <strong>social, cultural and safety demands</strong> (hospitality,
    etiquette, scald risk) — and you hold the task's full demand structure in your hands. The
    therapist then compares demand to the client's capacities, and two moves become available.
    <strong>Grading</strong> scales the demand up or down: a 300 ml travel kettle instead of the
    2 kg one, tea made seated, then standing, then carrying the tray; the same occupation
    becomes a precision training programme. <strong>Adapting</strong> changes the task or tools
    to match current capacity: a flip-top lid, a jar opener, pre-sliced bread, the kettle on the
    counter rather than overhead — restoring the occupation <em>today</em> while graded
    challenge rebuilds capacity for tomorrow. Good OT does both, and knows which it is doing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="sequence the analysis-to-intervention moves" widget="order" config={otAnalysisOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> adapting the task or handing over an aid means rehabilitation has failed — the "real" goal is restoring unaided movement. Grading and adapting are two tools aimed at two horizons: adaptation restores the occupation today, while graded challenge rebuilds capacity for tomorrow — and for many clients the honest clinical answer is that the environment, not the body, is the right thing to change. A therapist who treats every aid as defeat has confused the means (grip, range) with the end (the life the client wants back).</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — aids, grading and goals" widget="judge" config={otAidJudge} />
  <p>
    Two subtleties separate the textbook from practice. First, <strong>activity analysis is
    generic, occupational analysis is personal</strong>: the standard demands of "making tea"
    must be re-analysed for <em>this</em> client — their kitchen, their habits, their meaning
    (tea as ritual for a widow is not tea as caffeine delivery). Second, analysis runs forward
    and backward: forward, to find where a stroke survivor's left-side neglect breaks the chain
    (the forgotten milk, the scalded hand on the neglected side); backward, to design
    occupations that train the deficit — choosing dough-kneading precisely because it loads
    bilateral hand use, standing tolerance and sequencing at once. This is what lets one
    profession treat a child with cerebral palsy, a warehouse worker with a back injury and an
    elder with dementia: not by memorising a technique per diagnosis, but by choosing and tuning
    occupations against an analysed demand structure.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the two-teas problem" widget="branch" config={otTeaBranch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In occupational therapy, what does "occupation" mean, and why is it the core construct rather than the muscle or the diagnosis?</li>
    <li>Three patients each fail to dress after a stroke: one cannot, one will not, and one has had no morning structure since retiring. Which MOHO subsystem does each failure point to, and why does the distinction change the therapy?</li>
    <li>A delivery driver has lower-back pain. Name the three corners of the CMOP-E fit and give one legitimate intervention at each.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Occupation is everything people do to occupy themselves —
    self-care, productivity, leisure — and it is simultaneously the means (the therapeutic
    medium), the end (the goal the client names) and the measure (the outcome actually counted:
    can this person do what they need and love to do?). (2) The one who cannot points to
    performance capacity; the one who will not points to volition (perhaps dressing has become
    humiliation); the one with no structure points to habituation — three different problems
    with three different therapies. (3) Person (strengthen), occupation (re-teach lifting),
    environment (a trolley and a redesigned route) — three legitimate therapies for one problem,
    chosen by fit, evidence and the client's own priorities.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Occupation means everything people do to occupy themselves — self-care, productivity, leisure — not a job title.</li>
    <li>Occupation is simultaneously the means, the end and the measure of occupational therapy.</li>
    <li>MOHO explains why a person does not do (volition, habituation, performance capacity); CMOP-E locates the mismatch among person, occupation and environment.</li>
    <li>Activity analysis dissects an occupation into objects, sequence, body functions and social/safety demands — and occupational analysis re-runs it for this client.</li>
    <li>Grading scales the demand; adapting changes the task or tools — adaptation restores the occupation today while graded challenge rebuilds capacity for tomorrow.</li>
    <li>Occupational deprivation shows meaningful occupation behaves like a need, not a luxury — and goals are written as occupations, not impairments.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With the core construct in hand, the next lessons make it auditable: the practice
    framework's loop — occupational profile, goals written in occupations, interventions
    delivered through them, outcomes scored on the client's own ratings — turns the founding
    claim of 1917 into a repeatable method, and trials such as the Well Elderly studies supply
    the evidence that the doing, not just the exercising, is the active ingredient.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The models and the analysis feed a formal <strong>clinical process</strong> that turns the
    core construct into a repeatable professional method. The profession's practice framework
    (the OTPF, in its fourth edition) sequences the work: evaluation (the occupational profile —
    who is this person, what do they need and want to do — plus the analysis of performance),
    intervention planning (goals written in occupations, not impairments: "independently makes
    breakfast" rather than "improves grip strength"), intervention implementation (therapeutic
    use of occupations and activities, plus preparatory methods where needed), and outcome
    review against the client's own goals. The sequencing is the construct in motion: because
    the unit of concern is engagement, the goal-setting conversation happens with the client,
    the interventions are drawn from their actual occupations, and the success measure is
    their performance and satisfaction — which is what the Canadian Occupational Performance
    Measure scores, client-nominated and client-rated. Students sometimes mistake this loop
    for bureaucracy; it is more accurate to see it as the founding claim made auditable: if
    occupation is the active ingredient, then the process must begin with the client's
    occupations, deliver therapy through them, and count success in them.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the therapy goals come from the diagnosis — a stroke gets "the stroke protocol", whatever the person's life was. The practice framework runs the other way: the occupational profile asks who this person is and what they need and want to do before any impairment is named, because the same diagnosis lands in utterly different lives — the baker's arm and the chess player's arm. Goals written as occupations and scored on the client's own ratings are not a courtesy; they are the construct's audit trail.</p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet before you read on — where goals come from" widget="predict" config={otGoalPredict} />
  <p>
    Three directions carry the construct into current practice. <strong>Evidence</strong>: the
    strongest early trial support for the whole premise came from the Well Elderly studies
    (reported in JAMA, 1997, and replicated multi-site), in which preventive occupational therapy
    — culturally meaningful occupation, group-delivered — slowed functional decline in
    independent-living older adults, a result impossible to explain by strengthening alone and
    therefore pivotal for the "occupation as active ingredient" claim. <strong>Engagement versus
    performance</strong>: the 2007 CMOP-E extension is a quiet philosophical shift — the goal is
    not merely performing an occupation (which can be hollow, an outsider's checklist) but
    <em>engaging</em> in it, with the meaning intact; a client who bakes only for the
    therapist's camera has performed, not engaged, and the distinction changes how progress is
    scored. <strong>Systems and justice</strong>: occupation-centred thinking now runs upward
    from the individual to populations — occupational justice frameworks inform community
    programmes for refugees and the homeless, "ageing in place" redesigns housing stock, and
    return-to-work services treat job accommodation as an environmental intervention. And the
    open question the field keeps working at is measurement: meaning, balance and engagement
    resist the clean numbers of strength and range, so the profession's psychometric frontier —
    COPM, occupational balance scales, participation measures — is really the attempt to count
    the thing it cares about most. The core construct, though, has not moved since 1917: people
    are made of what they do, and therapy that rebuilds the doing rebuilds the person.
  </p>

